Describe that plot, and only that plot, as JSON so it can be redrawn cleanly. It is a
sketch, so capture its shape and labels, not precise data: choose x and y values on
whatever scale the axes show (or 0–10 if they show none) and give each curve enough
points (typically 10–30) to follow its shape, including peaks, kinks and asymptotes.

The JSON has this shape:

{
  "title": "title written above the plot, or null",
  "x_label": "x-axis label as written, or null",
  "y_label": "y-axis label as written, or null",
  "x_range": [min, max] or null,
  "y_range": [min, max] or null,
  "show_ticks": true only if the writer marked numbers on the axes,
  "x_log": true if the x-axis is marked as logarithmic,
  "y_log": true if the y-axis is marked as logarithmic,
  "curves": [
    {
      "label": "the curve's label or legend entry, or null",
      "kind": "line" or "scatter",
      "style": "solid", "dashed" or "dotted",
      "points": [[x, y], ...]
    }
  ],
  "annotations": [
    {"x": x, "y": y, "text": "a note written on the plot, placed where it was written"}
  ]
}

Use the writer's words exactly; do not correct spelling. If a word cannot be read,
write `[illegible]`; for an unsure guess, add `[?]`.

Reply with the JSON only: no preamble, no commentary, no code fences.
